WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.900
Why does cloud DNS need a deck of its own?

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.908
The workbook's DNS design
asks for one authoritative

3
00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:11.462
policy and state plane that
every other DNS system consumes.

4
00:00:11.462 --> 00:00:16.338
Cloud DNS services are synchronized
targets or forwarding destinations,

5
00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.775
not independently authored sources.

6
00:00:18.775 --> 00:00:22.250
The warning is about authoring
copies in every silo.

7
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:27.786
That produces split-brain answers, names
that work in one cloud but not another,

8
00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:30.512
and uneven Protective DNS and logging.

9
00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:34.787
The workbook already covers
Infoblox with Azure and AWS.

10
00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:37.618
It covered the Route 53 Resolver thinly,

11
00:00:37.618 --> 00:00:42.763
and Oracle Cloud only at the comparison
level, so Part 1 adds both in depth.

12
00:00:42.763 --> 00:00:46.250
BIND in a cloud wasn't
covered at all; that's Part 2.
